WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:02.043
Before Buffy Summers
and Indiana Jones,

00:00:02.127 --> 00:00:04.045
before Superman
and Ellen Ripley,

00:00:04.129 --> 00:00:06.715
before Robin Hood,
King Arthur and Beowulf,

00:00:06.798 --> 00:00:10.385
or Rey Skywalker, Achilles,
and Katniss Everdeen,

00:00:10.468 --> 00:00:12.721
there was the original
hero's journey

00:00:12.804 --> 00:00:15.765
and quite possibly the
oldest known story ever told,

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:17.684
"The Epic of Gilgamesh."

00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:20.979
Written around 5,000 years ago
in what is now Iraq,

00:00:21.062 --> 00:00:23.064
the Mesopotamian
myth of Gilgamesh

00:00:23.148 --> 00:00:24.649
has become the gold standard

00:00:24.733 --> 00:00:27.485
against which folklore
heroes have been measured.

00:00:27.569 --> 00:00:28.778
And his story contains many

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:30.947
of the foundational
tropes of mythology.

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:33.491
Even if you've never
read a Gilgamesh legend,

00:00:33.575 --> 00:00:36.327
his battles with monsters,
squabbles with gods,

00:00:36.411 --> 00:00:37.537
journeys to other worlds,

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.831
and general agonizing
about what it means

00:00:39.914 --> 00:00:42.250
to be a hero will
probably look familiar.

00:00:42.333 --> 00:00:45.003
But these storytelling devices
were cutting edge at the time

00:00:45.086 --> 00:00:47.505
and copied over and over again.

00:00:47.589 --> 00:00:49.174
Even though these
narrative beats hail

00:00:49.257 --> 00:00:51.801
from a time that feels
so unlike our own,

00:00:51.885 --> 00:00:55.054
Gilgamesh's inner journey
still feels relevant today.

00:00:55.138 --> 00:00:58.183
When we look closely at his
errors, his relationships,

00:00:58.266 --> 00:01:00.143
and his character
transformations,

00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:03.229
it's clear that he's so much
more than a mythical blueprint.

00:01:03.313 --> 00:01:05.815
[bright music]

00:01:07.859 --> 00:01:09.402
We first meet Gilgamesh

00:01:09.486 --> 00:01:13.364
as the beautiful brutal leader
of the Sumerian city of Uruk.

00:01:13.448 --> 00:01:15.158
He's kind of a toxic bro

00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:17.702
who likes getting into
brawls and seducing brides

00:01:17.786 --> 00:01:19.370
on their wedding nights.

00:01:19.454 --> 00:01:22.499
The goddess Aruru finally
gets sick of Gil's antics

00:01:22.582 --> 00:01:24.459
and mixes up some spit and clay

00:01:24.542 --> 00:01:28.588
to create a powerful adversary
for him, Harry Wildman.

00:01:28.671 --> 00:01:30.799
Oh, I'm sorry.
I read that wrong.

00:01:30.882 --> 00:01:35.094
I mean a hairy
wild man named Enkidu,

00:01:35.178 --> 00:01:37.972
sometimes depicted as
half bull, half man

00:01:38.056 --> 00:01:41.726
with superhuman strength, but
an innocent heart, like Gronk.

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:44.103
Enkidu skulks outside
the city walls,

00:01:44.187 --> 00:01:46.022
eating grass and
befriending animals,

00:01:46.105 --> 00:01:48.525
before striding into the
city to challenge Gilgamesh

00:01:48.608 --> 00:01:49.651
to a fight.

00:01:49.734 --> 00:01:50.693
The two appear to
be evenly matched

00:01:50.777 --> 00:01:52.612
as they wrestle
across the city,

00:01:52.695 --> 00:01:54.614
until Gilgamesh
eventually triumphs.

00:01:54.697 --> 00:01:58.034
After duking it out, their
rivalry turns to mutual respect.

00:02:00.745 --> 00:02:01.996
You're good, Gilgamesh.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:03.289
I'll give you that.

00:02:03.373 --> 00:02:05.542
You too, Enkidu. Hoo.

00:02:05.625 --> 00:02:06.876
Wanna truce?

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.336
Sure.

00:02:08.419 --> 00:02:11.631
You know what? Maybe we should,
like, join up forces.

00:02:11.714 --> 00:02:13.466
We'd probably be
better together.

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:14.634
Oh, I love that.

00:02:14.717 --> 00:02:16.511
You know, I've always
wanted a sidekick.

00:02:16.594 --> 00:02:18.847
Oh, great.

00:02:18.930 --> 00:02:20.223
Wait, what?

00:02:21.266 --> 00:02:22.517
Despite their differences,

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:25.478
from that day onwards,
the two are inseparable.

00:02:25.562 --> 00:02:27.313
Wait, that reminds me
of something.

00:02:27.397 --> 00:02:30.400
[encouraging music]

00:02:32.944 --> 00:02:35.113
Enkidu's innocence and
communion with nature

00:02:35.196 --> 00:02:38.616
make him a foil for Gilgamesh's
excessive tendencies,

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:40.743
but their relationship
is more intimate

00:02:40.827 --> 00:02:43.329
than your run-of-the-mill
hero-sidekick dynamic.

00:02:43.413 --> 00:02:45.456
You could call it
the original bromance.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:46.875
But many have viewed
Gilgamesh and Enkidu

00:02:46.958 --> 00:02:49.878
as a homoerotic or
queer love story

00:02:49.961 --> 00:02:52.005
in which the two
provide each other

00:02:52.088 --> 00:02:55.008
with much-needed
companionship and care.

00:02:55.091 --> 00:02:56.634
Their relationship is described

00:02:56.718 --> 00:02:59.178
as a marriage in numerous
passages of the epic,

00:02:59.262 --> 00:03:01.347
and it's the first time
we see Gilgamesh caring

00:03:01.431 --> 00:03:02.974
about someone
other than himself.

00:03:03.057 --> 00:03:05.602
Gilgamesh even confides
his deepest desire

00:03:05.685 --> 00:03:06.644
to his new companion:

00:03:06.728 --> 00:03:09.022
to achieve lasting fame.

00:03:09.105 --> 00:03:10.773
This was a lot harder
to do before YouTube.

00:03:10.857 --> 00:03:13.985
So together they embark on a
series of rebellious escapades.

00:03:14.068 --> 00:03:15.987
Chopping down
a sacred cedar forest,

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:17.906
murdering the many-headed
guardian of the cedars,

00:03:17.989 --> 00:03:21.367
and killing the
sacred Bull of Heaven.

00:03:21.451 --> 00:03:23.828
God forbid he should try to
get famous by, I don't know,

00:03:23.912 --> 00:03:27.206
planting a cedar forest
or healing a sacred bull.

00:03:27.290 --> 00:03:29.250
Well, the gods didn't
like it either.

00:03:29.334 --> 00:03:32.420
As punishment, they strike
Enkidu with a terminal illness

00:03:32.503 --> 00:03:34.839
and he dies in Gilgamesh's arms.

00:03:34.923 --> 00:03:36.049
Wait, what?

00:03:36.132 --> 00:03:37.467
Enkidu was the nice one.

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:41.054
All this chopping and killing
for fame was Gilgamesh's idea.

00:03:41.137 --> 00:03:43.473
But Enkidu's death
provides an opportunity

00:03:43.556 --> 00:03:44.974
for Gilgamesh to grow.

00:03:45.058 --> 00:03:46.559
How nice for Gilgamesh.

00:03:46.643 --> 00:03:49.312
He realizes he's been going
about things all wrong.

00:03:49.395 --> 00:03:52.273
His goal shouldn't be
fame, but immortality.

00:03:52.357 --> 00:03:53.983
Aim high, I guess.

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:55.610
Gilgamesh wanders
across the wilderness

00:03:55.693 --> 00:03:57.487
in search of Utnapishtim,

00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:00.531
a wise man who had been
granted immortality by the gods

00:04:00.615 --> 00:04:03.952
as a reward for saving humanity
from a cataclysmic flood

00:04:04.035 --> 00:04:05.495
by building a giant ark.

00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:07.455
What does that remind me of?

00:04:07.538 --> 00:04:08.665
Deucalion?

00:04:08.748 --> 00:04:09.749
No.

00:04:10.708 --> 00:04:11.876
Vivos vita?

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:12.961
No.

00:04:13.836 --> 00:04:14.837
Bergelmir?

00:04:14.921 --> 00:04:16.005
No.

00:04:17.048 --> 00:04:18.466
Forget it.

00:04:18.549 --> 00:04:22.095
To find Utnapishtim, Gilgamesh
must pass through Mashu,

00:04:22.178 --> 00:04:23.763
a mountain rooted
in the underworld

00:04:23.846 --> 00:04:25.682
and towering towards heaven.

00:04:25.765 --> 00:04:28.393
Its guardians, a pair
of scorpion warriors,

00:04:28.476 --> 00:04:31.521
warn him that no light touches
the passage through Mashu.

00:04:31.604 --> 00:04:34.816
After hours of total
darkness and howling winds,

00:04:34.899 --> 00:04:37.610
Gilgamesh emerges into
a glittering garden.

00:04:37.694 --> 00:04:39.654
Beyond the garden
lies the Sea of Death,

00:04:39.737 --> 00:04:42.490
and there on the shore is a bar.

00:04:42.573 --> 00:04:44.867
But instead of Tom Cruise
in a Hawaiian shirt,

00:04:44.951 --> 00:04:48.287
this bar is tended by
the divine alewife, Siduri.

00:04:48.371 --> 00:04:51.457
Like any good bartender, Siduri
listens to Gilgamesh's woes

00:04:51.541 --> 00:04:53.334
and gives him some smart advice.

00:04:53.418 --> 00:04:55.044
"Quit this wild goose chase

00:04:55.128 --> 00:04:58.047
and just enjoy what's left
of your short mortal life."

00:04:58.131 --> 00:05:00.591
Does that sound like something
our Gilgamesh would do?

00:05:00.675 --> 00:05:01.718
Heck no.

00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:04.512
He ignores her and sails
to Utnapishtim's island

00:05:04.595 --> 00:05:05.638
where he begs the old man

00:05:05.722 --> 00:05:07.807
for the secret to immortality.

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:09.809
Utnapishtim also thinks
Gil's mission is boneheaded,

00:05:09.892 --> 00:05:13.021
but, like a divine
reality show host,

00:05:13.104 --> 00:05:15.565
offers him a couple
physical challenges.

00:05:15.648 --> 00:05:18.484
First, if he can stay awake
for seven days straight,

00:05:18.568 --> 00:05:20.820
the gods might grant
him immortality.

00:05:20.903 --> 00:05:22.030
That's it?

00:05:22.113 --> 00:05:23.740
This is the guy who chopped
down the sacred cedars

00:05:23.823 --> 00:05:25.199
and hunted the Bull of Heaven.

00:05:25.283 --> 00:05:26.743
Staying awake should
be no problem at...

00:05:26.826 --> 00:05:29.662
Oh. Oh yeah, he falls asleep.

00:05:29.746 --> 00:05:32.457
Next, he must retrieve
a magical youth-giving plant

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:34.083
from the bottom of the ocean,

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:37.336
which gets snatched right out
of his hands by a snake.

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:38.880
Dejected and defeated,

00:05:38.963 --> 00:05:42.133
Gilgamesh finally gives up
on his dreams and heads home.

00:05:42.216 --> 00:05:44.761
But there, a profound
character shift occurs.

00:05:44.844 --> 00:05:46.304
The specifics vary depending

00:05:46.387 --> 00:05:47.388
on which version you're reading.

00:05:47.472 --> 00:05:49.932
Maybe he settles down
and starts a family.

00:05:50.016 --> 00:05:52.643
Maybe he focuses on being
a good leader and city builder,

00:05:52.727 --> 00:05:56.522
or maybe he pursues wisdom and
writes about his adventures.

00:05:56.606 --> 00:05:57.857
Whatever the details,

00:05:57.940 --> 00:06:00.109
Gilgamesh comes to accept
his human limitations

00:06:00.193 --> 00:06:03.071
and, like the gods had been
pushing him to do all along,

00:06:03.154 --> 00:06:05.865
make the most of his
limited time on earth.

00:06:05.948 --> 00:06:09.077
This is still a massively
popular narrative device.

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:10.912
How many heroes set off in
pursuit of fame and riches

00:06:10.995 --> 00:06:13.247
only to learn that
the real treasure

00:06:13.331 --> 00:06:15.208
is the friends we
made along the way?

00:06:15.291 --> 00:06:17.460
Gilgamesh set the
standard for real heroes

00:06:17.543 --> 00:06:20.088
knowing when to give up on
fame and glory and just...

00:06:22.048 --> 00:06:23.299
Let it go.

00:06:23.382 --> 00:06:24.884
Gilgamesh's decision to write

00:06:24.967 --> 00:06:27.595
about his life is also
a popular hero trope

00:06:27.678 --> 00:06:29.931
with particular meaning
for the Sumerian people.

00:06:30.014 --> 00:06:32.141
Gilgamesh's domain, Uruk,

00:06:32.225 --> 00:06:35.186
was a real place that grew
into a vast urban center

00:06:35.269 --> 00:06:36.896
during the 4th millennium BCE

00:06:36.979 --> 00:06:41.067
and is considered by many to
be the world's first real city.

00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:44.362
Bureaucracy and finance
increased the demand for writers

00:06:44.445 --> 00:06:46.239
who kept records and
tracked resources,

00:06:46.322 --> 00:06:48.574
but also put oral
stories to tablet.

00:06:48.658 --> 00:06:49.951
Written in cuneiform,

00:06:50.034 --> 00:06:51.702
the oldest known
form of writing,

00:06:51.786 --> 00:06:53.162
"The Epic of Gilgamesh" is one

00:06:53.246 --> 00:06:54.872
of the earliest
works of literature

00:06:54.956 --> 00:06:57.125
that testifies in numerous ways

00:06:57.208 --> 00:06:59.836
to the enduring human
desire to make our mark.

00:06:59.919 --> 00:07:03.422
In that way, Gilgamesh did
kind of achieve immortality.

00:07:03.506 --> 00:07:05.633
Maybe instead of sending him
to the bottom of the sea,

00:07:05.716 --> 00:07:08.719
Utnapishtim should have just
handed him a chisel and tablet

00:07:08.803 --> 00:07:10.138
and sent him home.

00:07:10.221 --> 00:07:12.098
Now it's time for another
one of our Pantheon Picks.

00:07:12.181 --> 00:07:14.267
This is something new
we're doing this season.

00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:16.727
Dr. Z and I are each going
to choose different figures

00:07:16.811 --> 00:07:18.604
from mythology that we love

00:07:18.688 --> 00:07:20.189
and we're going to join them

00:07:20.273 --> 00:07:23.025
in a united pantheon at
the end of this season.

00:07:23.109 --> 00:07:24.277
I made my first pick

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:25.653
in the first episode
of the season,

00:07:25.736 --> 00:07:28.030
and now it is Dr. Z's turn.

00:07:28.114 --> 00:07:30.533
For a Mesopotamian
mythology episode

00:07:30.616 --> 00:07:33.119
and probably the least
surprising pick of all time,

00:07:33.202 --> 00:07:34.954
I'm going with Tiamat.

00:07:35.037 --> 00:07:35.997
What?

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:38.332
I know! Shocking from
the monster expert.

00:07:38.416 --> 00:07:40.918
Tiamat is considered
a mother by some people now,

00:07:41.002 --> 00:07:44.380
but she was a creation
deity originally.

00:07:44.463 --> 00:07:46.716
She literally housed
all of creation

00:07:46.799 --> 00:07:48.926
in Mesopotamian
mythology in her womb.

00:07:49.010 --> 00:07:51.095
And I think like a warm,
squishy waterbed

00:07:51.179 --> 00:07:53.222
of creation sounds
like a nice option

00:07:53.306 --> 00:07:54.974
to have to relax in the utopia.

00:07:55.057 --> 00:07:56.392
She can create life,

00:07:56.475 --> 00:07:59.145
and also could help us
defending the utopia

00:07:59.228 --> 00:08:01.230
against anyone
trying to attack us.

00:08:01.314 --> 00:08:02.523
If we need to make

00:08:02.607 --> 00:08:04.150
some kind of weird
Pegasus-weather demon hybrid,

00:08:04.233 --> 00:08:05.484
she's got the experience.

00:08:05.568 --> 00:08:07.445
Oh, brilliant choice.
I love that.

00:08:07.528 --> 00:08:08.654
And you're right,
I'm not surprised

00:08:08.738 --> 00:08:11.532
but your justification
is perfect, immaculate,

00:08:11.616 --> 00:08:12.533
great choice.

00:08:12.617 --> 00:08:13.618
(Emily)
Thank you.

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:18.831
Before you go, PBS has
several new documentary series

00:08:18.915 --> 00:08:20.458
over on PBS Voices.

00:08:20.541 --> 00:08:22.460
And if you haven't seen
them, now's the time.

00:08:22.543 --> 00:08:25.171
"Ritual" explores the
history of unique customs

00:08:25.254 --> 00:08:26.672
from across the American South.

00:08:26.756 --> 00:08:28.007
"Dead and Buried"

00:08:28.090 --> 00:08:29.967
reexamines our society's
relationship with death

00:08:30.051 --> 00:08:31.052
and "Brave Spaces" spotlights

00:08:31.135 --> 00:08:33.846
LGBTQ communities
standing strong

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:35.598
in the face of adversity.

00:08:35.681 --> 00:08:37.058
We've got links in
the description.

00:08:37.141 --> 00:08:40.061
We hope you check 'em out and
let us know what you think.

00:08:40.144 --> 00:08:40.770
(director)
Or, sorry. Right.

00:08:41.938 --> 00:08:44.148
-Okay.
-Let's start from the top.

00:08:44.232 --> 00:08:45.233
[singing]
Let it go!

00:08:46.234 --> 00:08:48.027
(director)
I mean, even though
you won, Gilgamesh...

00:08:48.110 --> 00:08:49.111
Okay, cut.

